```
Please note that C# Debug.Assert does not function the same way as other languages such as Java or Python. In C#, Debug.Assert does not halt the program execution unless it's compiled with a /debug or /checked compiler option and a debugger is attached. Instead, it works by evaluating the condition in the debug mode and discarding the condition and the evaluated code in the release mode.

To use Debug.Assert, you must ensure there is a valid debugging environment. In Visual Studio, the program execution can be halted when a Debug.Assert condition is met by pressing F5 or going to Debug -> Start Debugging or Ctrl+F5 to start without debugging.

In your case, the assertion has been put in comments for the PUT_RemoveContract method because the "@@@ index of x in new array is geq minus 1" is not met by the current code.
